{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules","id":1476949,"title":"Anti-Revised Metaplot rules","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-03-22T05:19:19-07:00","id":1476949,"title":"Anti-Revised Metaplot rules","content":"My overwhelming dislike (no Edition Warring intended, I am just trying to describe my purpose) of the MRev Metaplot and the playstyle that it favored had long since driven me to prefer optional and/or house rules and setting changes that may nullify its effects. Of late, I have grown wondering how best to apply them properly in fine detail.<br /> <br /> I fancy the notion of human-like supernaturals such as mages and changelings being as resilient to lethal damage as vampires and shapeshifters, and I am not entirely averse to the notion of the sturdiest mortals sharing in the benefit. However, I also like the idea of supernaturals, no matter their kind or physique, being more resilient than mortals by default, and grandmothers not being able to soak shotgun blasts. Therefore, I have tentatively thought of making Cinematic Damage the default, but modifying it to make all mages and changelings, and humans with Stamina 3+, able to soak lethal. Setting rationale is that Awakening or having an active fae soul strenghtens the body just as being a vampire or shapeshifter, if in a different way. Speaking in flavor and symbolic terms, all mages have Too Tough to Die at no cost in these terms.<br /> <br /> About Acclimation and Disembodiment, I dislike them just as much, but I find M20 treatment of them confusing. The book acknowledges Acclimation was caused and sustained by the Avatar Storm, and so was accelerated Disembodiment. However, it also retroactively grandfathers a lesser form of Disembodiment as always having existed. As far as I can remember, however, Disembodiment was never a thing in1st/2nd Edition. Therefore, I have tentatively thought of letting all mages, or at least the ones whose paradigm acknowledges the existence of the Otherworlds (mystics, Etherites, Virtual Adepts, Void Engineers, etc.), have Umbral Affinity at no cost. The mages whose paradigm makes the Umbra a big deal, as well as the ones with an inherent connection to the Umbra such as being a Natural Channel, Nephilim/Laham, Deathwalker, etc. also have that, plus the equivalent of Shapeshifter Kin (without the actual blood ties) at no cost. I am conflicted at doing away with Acclimation and Disembodiment entirely.<br /> <br /> The Avatar Storm never existed, or it was a brief and minor event. Its destructive effects get partially replaced by other setting changes, without restricting access to the Umbra. Speaking in flavor and symbolic terms, all mages have Stormwarden at no cost.<br /> <br /> Vulgar without Witnesses does not exist, immortality spells do not cause Permadox, and mages roll Arete + Sphere to cast magick as in DA:M and M: tSC. Setting rationale is the Consensus never got strong enough to enforce Paradox without the support of active and present Sleeper disbelief or the mage bungling the spell, or weaken magick casting.<br /> <br /> Consequently, immortality without Paradox trouble is the default state of sufficiently powerful and experienced mages, unless they somehow do not want to be or fail to avoid the hazards of their lifestyle. Elder mages play as relevant a role in supernatural society as elder vampires, but in both cases, other setting changes prevent supernatural society from getting too static.<br /> <br /> Opnions? Suggestions?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-22-2022, 08:24 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T06:04:30-07:00","id":1476951,"title":"","content":"It feels like going beyond just undoing the Revised metaplot and the rules it brought along with that (M20 lets you do that easily enough).<br /> <br /> Mages not having special soak rules without using magic, vulgar without witnesses, immortality spells not being perfect, low casting pools, and all of that were part of the game even in the height of 2e gonzo craziness.<br /> <br /> I have no idea why you think the high end masters/archmasters like the pre-Avatar Storm Council of Nine, Porthos, or Voormas need to get more powerful than they already are. What does it add to the game for these characters to be even more powerful? They're already way beyond what &quot;elder&quot; usually entails in VtM and frankly beyond most methuselahs too.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T08:16:29-07:00","id":1476963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules#post1476949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">About Acclimation and Disembodiment, I dislike them just as much, but I find M20 treatment of them confusing. The book acknowledges Acclimation was caused and sustained by the Avatar Storm, and so was accelerated Disembodiment. However, it also retroactively grandfathers a lesser form of Disembodiment as always having existed. As far as I can remember, however, Disembodiment was never a thing in1st/2nd Edition.</div> </div> </div> </div>M20 was written by <i>the</i> premier expert on the first two editions of Mage. If he says that there was a lesser version of Disembodiment before MRev, you can trust that there was. And in fact, there was: <br /> <div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>Disconnection:</b> \u2026 Explorers without shapeshifter blood eventually lose their connection to the Earth if they stay in the Umbra for long. After a while (one to four months for most people, four months to a year for primal mysticks), they forget their lives on Earth and become spirits. Their physical bodies fall away (or die, in the case of astral travelers) and their consciousness shifts them into spirits which embody their true nature. This is a one-way process. \u2026 People who dwell in Horizon Realms don't suffer this effect, but gradually take on the aspects of the Realm in which they live, and they shape that Realm in return.<br /> <br /> <b>Book of Worlds</b> p.183<br /> </div><br /> I personally have some difficulty wrapping my head around the notion that \u201cthree months\u201d (MRev) is less than \u201cone to four months\u201d (M2e); but there you go.<br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> The bottom line, as far as I'm concerned, is that the writer of M20 took great pains to highlight what the differences between M2 and MRev are, and to give you the choice between the two. If there's a lack of understanding of anything on his part, it was of some of the MRev material. You shouldn't need to house-rule M20 <i>at all</i> to get something that's in accordance with M2. <br /> <br /> And as Heavy Arms points out, a number of your musings here aren't really about \u201canti-Revised\u201d rules; they're more \u201canti all-editions\u201d. But, like I said in another thread, you do you. <br /> <br /> With that said, you might want to consider adopting a design concept from <b>Mage: the Awakening 2e</b>, namely the concept of Attainments. This idea is that as you progress through the Arcana (Awakening's counterpart to the Spheres), you gradually become more inherently magical, and develop some innate abilities that serve as permanent Effects. Some of these are just a case of formalizing particular rule quirks, such as needing one dot in an Arcanum in order to counterspell Effects created by that Arcanum, or the fact that two dots of Space (i.e., Correspondence) lets you use the Sympathetic Range chart. But others are more drastic: the first dot in an Arcanum grants Mage Sight, meaning that any supernal phenomena involving that Arcanum is mystically highlighted for you; no casting needed. And the second dot grants Mage Armor, providing Arcanum-appropriate protection. <br /> <br /> I could see implementing an M20 counterpart to this, where anyone with two dots of Life gets to soak lethal damage; although compared to the various benefits that Awakening's Attainments provide, that's actually quite mild.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T08:52:18-07:00","id":1476966,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1476951#post1476951\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It feels like going beyond just undoing the Revised metaplot and the rules it brought along with that (M20 lets you do that easily enough).</div> </div> </div> </div>In fact, the house rules I devised in certain cases are modifications of M20 optional ones (e.g. lethal soak, Acclimation, and Disembodiment) to suit my preferences. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Mages not having special soak rules without using magic, vulgar without witnesses, immortality spells not being perfect, low casting pools, and all of that were part of the game even in the height of 2e gonzo craziness. </div> </div> </div>Well, my playstyle preferences and consequent rules/setting hacks are no necessarily limited to just undoing or nullifying MRev stuff. The other changes you quote are just as cherished in my book, so I take the opportunity to fold everything in a neat package. E.g. removing Vw/oW and having efficient immortality spells have been my preference pretty much since the first time I cracked open the MtA rulebook. I may point out that I took inspiration for using Arete + Sphere (and different Paradox rules) from DA:M and MtSC. I agree that in a setting similar to canon some kind of constraint to keep overt magic from the gazes of Sleepers may be appropriate, although my version of the setting is kinda different to begin with.<br /> <br /> However, I see no redeeming purpose in my playstyle or story preferences for Vw/oW (I much prefere magic to be flashy rather than subtle when Sleepers are not around), making mages or changelings more frail to blades or bullets than vampires or werewolves, and successful mages being as long-lived as successful vampires. I may also point out that in my preferred version of the oWoD setting, the Ascension War turned Hot and engulfed the other Night-Folk. Most supernaturals reorganized into Triatic uber-factions that fight a no-holds barred war for reality that makes 2e gonzo craziness look tame. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I have no idea why you think the high end masters/archmasters like the pre-Avatar Storm Council of Nine, Porthos, or Voormas need to get more powerful than they already are. What does it add to the game for these characters to be even more powerful? They're already way beyond what &quot;elder&quot; usually entails in VtM and frankly beyond most methuselahs too. </div> </div> </div>Well, I fancy the most powerful mages looking godlike, and in the Reality War, the Tradition luminaries turn rather useful to keep the Entropic hordes at bay. The Nephandi Lords make most methuselahs look like babies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T09:20:54-07:00","id":1476971,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1476963#post1476963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">M20 was written by <i>the</i> premier expert on the first two editions of Mage. If he says that there was a lesser version of Disembodiment before MRev, you can trust that there was. And in fact, there was:<div style=\"margin-left:40px\"><b>Disconnection:</b> \u2026 Explorers without shapeshifter blood eventually lose their connection to the Earth if they stay in the Umbra for long. After a while (one to four months for most people, four months to a year for primal mysticks), they forget their lives on Earth and become spirits. Their physical bodies fall away (or die, in the case of astral travelers) and their consciousness shifts them into spirits which embody their true nature. This is a one-way process. \u2026 People who dwell in Horizon Realms don't suffer this effect, but gradually take on the aspects of the Realm in which they live, and they shape that Realm in return.<br /> <br /> <b>Book of Worlds</b> p.183</div>I personally have some difficulty wrapping my head around the notion that \u201cthree months\u201d (MRev) is less than \u201cone to four months\u201d (M2e); but there you go.<br /> <hr /><br /> The bottom line, as far as I'm concerned, is that the writer of M20 took great pains to highlight what the differences between M2 and MRev are, and to give you the choice between the two. If there's a lack of understanding of anything on his part, it was of some of the MRev material. You shouldn't need to house-rule M20 <i>at all</i> to get something that's in accordance with M2.<br /> <br /> And as Heavy Arms points out, a number of your musings here aren't really about \u201canti-Revised\u201d rules; they're more \u201canti all-editions\u201d. But, like I said in another thread, you do you.<br /> <br /> With that said, you might want to consider adopting a design concept from <b>Mage: the Awakening 2e</b>, namely the concept of Attainments. This idea is that as you progress through the Arcana (Awakening's counterpart to the Spheres), you gradually become more inherently magical, and develop some innate abilities that serve as permanent Effects. Some of these are just a case of formalizing particular rule quirks, such as needing one dot in an Arcanum in order to counterspell Effects created by that Arcanum, or the fact that two dots of Space (i.e., Correspondence) lets you use the Sympathetic Range chart. But others are more drastic: the first dot in an Arcanum grants Mage Sight, meaning that any supernal phenomena involving that Arcanum is mystically highlighted for you; no casting needed. And the second dot grants Mage Armor, providing Arcanum-appropriate protection.<br /> <br /> I could see implementing an M20 counterpart to this, where anyone with two dots of Life gets to soak lethal damage; although compared to the various benefits that Awakening's Attainments provide, that's actually quite mild.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well, I yield to your superior expertise. That quotation had escaped my memory, perhaps because the relevant rule was not as notable in the system as Disembodiment is in M20. I agree the canon M20 duration for these rules look kinda iffy, which is yet another reason why I am trying to raise the scale and make it more uniform (and comprehensive: IMO there are other kinds of mages that are just as entitled as being resistent to Disembodiments as the Kin). Being a little creatively challenged, I could not think of a better unifying theme for my bag of houserules and a title for the thread than being anti-MRev (since that system tends to make mages street-level, low-powered, subtle, combat-shy, and down to earth, and my purpose is just the opposite). I agree what I am trying to do goes beyond the strict 2nd/MRev/M20 difference, and I am not entirely satisfied with how even M20 optional rules (or canon ones for casting and paradox) manage things. E.g. I am determined to make mages and changelings different from mortals about how they withstand damage. I am satisfied with how M20 manages various other things (e.g. Foci rules have been a massive improvement that give room to customize things to my preferences).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T14:16:48-07:00","id":1477019,"title":"","content":"If you want to go way beyond &quot;anti-Revised&quot; that's cool, but it's hard to just respond to the OP without much of the later (or external) context.<br /> <br /> Beyond that... I really don't know how you maintain the setting as anything like it is. Mages are barely held back from steamrolling most of the other things in the WoD as it is. The more you chip away at mages' weaknesses, the less sense the setting makes. At some point the idea of mages caring about hiding themselves from Sleeper gets pointless if the only thing holding them back is active Sleeper disbelief (and how long would that last with Mind masters free to garner hundreds of successes to shift consensus in their local area to create friendly reality zones?).<br /> <br /> I think one of the reasons to not ignore the suggestion of looking at CofD for Mage and Changeling is not to necessarily change the basic rules you want to run with, but look at ideas for different approaches to these character types with different weaknesses that help keep them in line with the mood of the WoD than how Ascension has tried to keep mages in line.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T15:25:20-07:00","id":1477029,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477019#post1477019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you want to go way beyond &quot;anti-Revised&quot; that's cool, but it's hard to just respond to the OP without much of the later (or external) context.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand and apologize for my difficulty in deciding how much context to give about my rules and setting hacks when I post about more spefic topics, since they are kind of extensive. I suppose I might set up a Googledoc with the summary of my houserules and setting hacks and link to that in the future.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Beyond that... I really don't know how you maintain the setting as anything like it is. Mages are barely held back from steamrolling most of the other things in the WoD as it is. The you chip away at mages' weaknesses, the less sense the setting makes. At some point the idea of mages caring about hiding themselves from Sleeper gets pointless if the only thing holding them back is active Sleeper disbelief (and how long would that last with Mind masters free to garner hundreds of successes to shift consensus in their local area to create friendly reality zones?). </div> </div> </div>Fact is, I do not, as I explained in #4. I like a lot of things in the two Mage games, but I feel an irresistible drive to make magic flashy and OP by default, except possibly when hiding from Sleepers, and even that at sufferance. Subtle and street-level stuff annoy and bore me to tears.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T16:16:33-07:00","id":1477030,"title":"","content":"I mean, magic is flashy and overpowered by default... that's why the system has a bunch of consequence oriented drawbacks to encourage mages to not be flashy in an urban fantasy base-setting.<br /> <br /> But like... what's the point of even keeping to the WoD? It's not that great of a system (esp not 30 years on) and tossing out the urban fantasy tropes/assumptions to the level you are means the past 500 years of the setting would have diverged from our world so wildly you could just run Exalted with Spheres instead of Charms and it would probably make more sense than somehow we have something close to the real world with a magical reality war happening behind the scenes. Or at least Scion 2e as a base with Sphere magic instead of Purviews... Scion 2e already assumes a world that fundamentally not ours even if it tries hard to explain how it's a lot more like ours than it could have ended up.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T17:29:09-07:00","id":1477040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477030#post1477030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I mean, magic is flashy and overpowered by default... that's why the system has a bunch of consequence oriented drawbacks to encourage mages to not be flashy in an urban fantasy base-setting.<br /> <br /> But like... what's the point of even keeping to the WoD? It's not that great of a system (esp not 30 years on) and tossing out the urban fantasy tropes/assumptions to the level you are means the past 500 years of the setting would have diverged from our world so wildly you could just run Exalted with Spheres instead of Charms and it would probably make more sense than somehow we have something close to the real world with a magical reality war happening behind the scenes. Or at least Scion 2e as a base with Sphere magic instead of Purviews... Scion 2e already assumes a world that fundamentally not ours even if it tries hard to explain how it's a lot more like ours than it could have ended up.</div> </div> </div> </div>Fact is, in making a value judgement on a RPG I tend to focus on the powers first and foremost and then branch out to the rest of the system. I love the flashy, OP, and freeform character of games like M20, DA:M, DA:F, MtSC, MtAw, Ars Magica, C20, etc. Spell-list systems such as Exalted or Scion bore me to tears and make my eyes cross after a few pages, and tend to leave me cold and dismissive about features in the rest of the game. Due to my priorities I find more congenial to hack something like the WoD to remove the Masquerade element than something like Exalted to transplant a wholly different power system. Or for that matter hack the fantasy setting of DA:M, DA:F, and/or MtSC to ensure Tradition mages or fae are no longer doomed.<br /> <br /> As much as I fancy reading or watching urban fantasy stuff, I usually focus on the Unmasqued World portion of the genre and ignore the Masquerade stuff. When cultural osmosis forces me to do otherwise, e.g. with Harry Potter, I find myself thinking that Grindelwald Was Right all the time. It is the same reason why in the superhero genre I prefer to focus on characters that spurn secret identities and choose to live the hero or villain lifestyle in plain sight 24/7, and stories that are not shy to let the influence of superhumans change society. I do not get the point or appeal at all of the secrecy or trying to keep the world a close copy of our own. One of the many things I love about the MCU is almost nobody has a secret identity and important sci-fi or fantasy elements are changing the world.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-23-2022, 06:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-22T18:07:49-07:00","id":1477042,"title":"","content":"Scion 2e is hardly a &quot;spell list&quot; system. Knacks are, but those are minor little tricks that are more like everyone getting a few 5-7 dot supernatural Merits. Purviews in 2e literally can do anything, and the individual powers are more like rotes in Mage: refined uses of divine power that your character is esp, good at. Anyone with the Sky Purview and zap you with a thunderbolt, but not everyone is Zeus that's got it as a standard part of his thing. It really seems more like what you want that I think you might realize.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T02:40:45-07:00","id":1477066,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477042#post1477042\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Scion 2e is hardly a &quot;spell list&quot; system. Knacks are, but those are minor little tricks that are more like everyone getting a few 5-7 dot supernatural Merits. Purviews in 2e literally can do anything, and the individual powers are more like rotes in Mage: refined uses of divine power that your character is esp, good at. Anyone with the Sky Purview and zap you with a thunderbolt, but not everyone is Zeus that's got it as a standard part of his thing. It really seems more like what you want that I think you might realize.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> We shall see. It is hard for me to make a definite value judgement on a game until I have all the important pieces of the system (rulebooks, player's guides, sourcebooks) on my desk or screen to tinker, customize, and min-max to my heart's content, and we are less than halfway with releases of Scion 2e in this regard. However, I remember I once or twice voiced a few important concerns and ideas of mine about the preferred OP power level (I know that Origin and Hero are not the right stage for that, but IIRC I was considering how to prepare the game to evolve that way), complex character concepts with generalist power sets, and ability to use powers w/o relics and in a freeform way frequently and reliably, and I was told in no uncertain way on the Scion forum that my preferences and expectations made Scion 2e not the right game for me. I do not remember the details. Therefore, my current attitude towards Scion 2e is indifferent to negative. I have supported all the kickstarter campaigns, but more out of a completist urge than anything else.<br /> <br /> Frankly, the innovative stuff that got other people so enthusiastic about the new versions of Scion and Aberrant (nothing in Trinity ever appealed to me much, but I love a lot of things in any version of Aberrant, although it is far from perfect either way) tends to be of little importance to me since I do not really need the system to hold my hands in this regard. Ability to customize and use the powers to my preferences is much more important in my priority scale.<br /> <br /> To expand on the relics point, ability to use powers efficiently and reliably w/o dependence on tools is another very important gaming need of mine. If my characters are not able to use 90-95% of their abilities (especially the combat-worthy stuff) effectively when thrown naked in an empty cell, I feel quite annoyed and frustrated. The &quot;Are you the god of hammers?&quot; scene of Thor: Ragnarok had me nodding and cheering enthusiastically.<br /> <br /> One reason I love M20 is the ample freedom of customization it offers in this regard. My preferred concepts invariably turn out to be mystics with Foci that are almost entirely based on actions rather than tools and can be used in the field quickly and efficiently. It is one reason why my interest in Technocracy stuff or ritual magic is almost non-existent. For the Technocracy, of course, there is also the fact that almost everything in the faction alienates me; I do not get the appeal of playing a Man in Black at all.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-23-2022, 06:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T05:36:25-07:00","id":1477078,"title":"","content":"You say you've been backing all of the Kickstarters, right? Does <b>Scion: Demigod</b> scratch the itch for you? Where the original Scion: Demigod was essentially just Scion: Hero with more rungs on the ladder, the new version installs an elevator: the Dominion systems greatly increase the ease with which a Scion can do the miraculous. For example: in many cases where Heroes had to spend Legend to temporarily gain a benefit (such as Marvels), Demigods get to imbue Legend, making those same benefits all but permanent. Casual Miracles effectively lets you perform (relatively) low-level Marvels on a whim. Dominion Stunts grant you and your followers added abilities for a whole scene, with the only \u201ccost\u201d being the successes on a Skill roll to bring them into play. And so on.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T06:01:23-07:00","id":1477079,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477066#post1477066\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To expand on the relics point, ability to use powers efficiently and reliably w/o dependence on tools is another very important gaming need of mine. If my characters are not able to use 90-95% of their abilities (especially the combat-worthy stuff) effectively when thrown naked in an empty cell, I feel quite annoyed and frustrated. The &quot;Are you the god of hammers?&quot; scene of Thor: Ragnarok had me nodding and cheering enthusiastically.</div> </div> </div> </div>OK...<br /> <br /> A Hero level Scion has constant access to:<br /> <br /> 1) Knacks (baring ones that link to specific things, but just avoid thus if that's not your thing, there aren't a lot of them).<br /> 2) Legend and Feats of Scale tied to their Legendary Title(s).<br /> 3) Two innate Purviews (their Pantheon Purview and one of their parent's Purviews) with their associated innate bonuses, and whatever Boons you take (nothing stops you from keeping your Boons within these two Purviews), and the ability to use them to create Marvels.<br /> <br /> They can get more powers via Guides and Relics (and bonuses from Birthrights in general), but those powers are limited by access to them. Just like mage can't use a Wonder if it's not on hand. While Scion 2e provides considerable benefits to taking more Purviews and Boons through this method, they're not mandatory.<br /> <br /> At Demigod, their Purviews get a huge upgrade (we already know what this looks like). The only think that would at all seem lacking to your interests in getting more innate Purviews since that seems to be saved for completely apotheosis. Dominion, Divine Dice, and Casual Miracles are all fairly big deals.<br /> <br /> The only problem I can really see for your stated preferences is that Demigod keeps things to two innate Purviews, and the restrictions on which those are; but if you feel like Scion 2e demigods aren't somehow powerful enough, having them build up their innate Pureviews over the journey from Legend 5 to 9 instead of waiting for 9 to hit them with yet another massive jump in power isn't hard to do. It would pretty much necessitate moments like the MCU Thor reference as part of the apotheosis arc Demigods go through.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> One reason I love M20 is the ample freedom of customization it offers in this regard. </div> </div> </div>This rings somewhat hollow to me. You love M20 because... you can ignore so much of it instead of just playing games that actually suit the kind of power level you want to play? That's the point of investing in such a customization heavy system if you're only in it to build the same thing every time?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T06:35:29-07:00","id":1477082,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477078#post1477078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You say you've been backing all of the Kickstarters, right? Does <b>Scion: Demigod</b> scratch the itch for you? Where the original Scion: Demigod was essentially just Scion: Hero with more rungs on the ladder, the new version installs an elevator: the Dominion systems greatly increase the ease with which a Scion can do the miraculous. For example: in many cases where Heroes had to spend Legend to temporarily gain a benefit (such as Marvels), Demigods get to imbue Legend, making those same benefits all but permanent. Casual Miracles effectively lets you perform (relatively) low-level Marvels on a whim. Dominion Stunts grant you and your followers added abilities for a whole scene, with the only \u201ccost\u201d being the successes on a Skill roll to bring them into play. And so on.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I have been backing all of the Kickstarters out a completist urge, but ATM I am disillusioned enough about Scion 2e not to pay much attention to its development cycle, so I know next to nothing about Demigod or the other books in the pipeline. I might revisit the issue once the whole set is available. Min-maxer me dislikes dealing with an incomplete set of tools. Even with M20, I did not really started tinkering with the game until the whole basic set (M20, BoS, HYDT, GaM) was available.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T07:02:37-07:00","id":1477085,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477079#post1477079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> OK...<br /> <br /> A Hero level Scion has constant access to:<br /> <br /> 1) Knacks (baring ones that link to specific things, but just avoid thus if that's not your thing, there aren't a lot of them).<br /> 2) Legend and Feats of Scale tied to their Legendary Title(s).<br /> 3) Two innate Purviews (their Pantheon Purview and one of their parent's Purviews) with their associated innate bonuses, and whatever Boons you take (nothing stops you from keeping your Boons within these two Purviews), and the ability to use them to create Marvels.<br /> <br /> They can get more powers via Guides and Relics (and bonuses from Birthrights in general), but those powers are limited by access to them. Just like mage can't use a Wonder if it's not on hand. While Scion 2e provides considerable benefits to taking more Purviews and Boons through this method, they're not mandatory.<br /> <br /> At Demigod, their Purviews get a huge upgrade (we already know what this looks like). The only think that would at all seem lacking to your interests in getting more innate Purviews since that seems to be saved for completely apotheosis. Dominion, Divine Dice, and Casual Miracles are all fairly big deals.<br /> <br /> The only problem I can really see for your stated preferences is that Demigod keeps things to two innate Purviews, and the restrictions on which those are; but if you feel like Scion 2e demigods aren't somehow powerful enough, having them build up their innate Pureviews over the journey from Legend 5 to 9 instead of waiting for 9 to hit them with yet another massive jump in power isn't hard to do. It would pretty much necessitate moments like the MCU Thor reference as part of the apotheosis arc Demigods go through.</div> </div> </div> </div>IIRC the one Pantheon Purview that had any interest for me was the Theoi one, since I love shapeshifting. Having to channel all of my powers through a single Purview or two is a serious red flag for me since another gaming thing I hate is power specialization and thematic constraint. As much as I fancy min-maxing in other matters, I love power variety, flexibility, and redundance. A character of mine tends to have at least a half-dozen broad power types, with at least half of being good for combat uses in different or redundant ways. I am rarely satisfied to throw fireballs, although it is almost a prerequisite; I want to use fireballs, lightning bolts, ice bolts, super-strong punches, and TK crushes or throws.<br /> <br /> E.g. my ideal superhuman (assuming we can treat Scions as such) is OP, immortal, a flying brick, a generalist elementalist, an energy projector, a healer and/or a Wolvie-style regenerator, a telekinetic, and a shapeshifter. They are also able to do all of that more or less at leisure w/o any reliance on tools (except possibly for an additional power boost and/or finesse focus) or special activation conditions on a limited budget when going beyond a narrow thematic focus. Having to rely on a juice pool for any serious power use on equal terms for different abilities may or may not be acceptable depending on circumstances (i.e. size of the pool and difficulty to recharge).<br /> <br /> If they were to be a God, and not so rarely have been in appropriate ttrpgs and cyoas, they would be the God of Strength/Might (or any other Domain necessary for all-around physical prowess), Life, Fire/Sun, and Sky/Weather. I may often add Water, Earth, Magic, Knowledge, Love/Lust, and/or Trickery to round up the portofolio and expand it beyond the core concept.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This rings somewhat hollow to me. You love M20 because... you can ignore so much of it instead of just playing games that actually suit the kind of power level you want to play? That's the point of investing in such a customization heavy system if you're only in it to build the same thing every time? </div> </div> </div>Well, what can I say? I suppose I am the gaming equivalent of those authors that prefer to create endless variations of the same few concepts and stories. I like developing a few optimized templates and concepts that reflect my preferences satisfactorily, then build endless variations of them in different games. I suppose it is the result of gaming often being a self-insert wish fulfilment for me, having a serious optimizer streak, and being a &quot;my way or the highway&quot; individualist.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-23-2022, 11:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T07:34:53-07:00","id":1477088,"title":"","content":"Demigod addresses your \u201crequires juice\u201d complaint in at least three different ways. <br /> <br /> There's Investment of Legend, where the whole spend-and-recover paradigm goes out the window. <br /> <br /> There are the Dominion Stunts, which build on the concept of Twists of Fate in that they're stunts and therefore \u201cfueled\u201d by successes. <br /> <br /> There are Casual Miracles, which are things that Demigods can do without <i>any</i> sorry of expenditure, investment, or dice pool; they just do these things whenever they feel like it. <br /> <br /> <hr /><br /> Basically, I've told you that the thing you're complaining about has been addressed; yet you're still talking like it hasn't been. That can get irritating.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T13:48:05-07:00","id":1477116,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477085#post1477085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having to channel all of my powers through a single Purview or two is a serious red flag for me since another gaming thing I hate is power specialization and thematic constraint.</div> </div> </div> </div>Serious question: Do you care about the utility of the game as a group activity? Because a lot of your dislikes seem to be around things that ensure everyone playing has their own things.<br /> <br /> It also feels very strange to hate on thematic constraints when that's pretty much what 99% of Mage is built on..<br /> <br /> Purviews are extremely broad powers. They don't have dot ratings like Spheres. Once you get access to a Purview you get the whole package. Marvels from one Purview can copy any power from any other Purview with the minor thematic constraint of, &quot;explain how it fits one of your available Motifs.&quot; In Ascension, you have to explain how an effect fits your Focus just to us the powers you already have, Scion 2e only makes you do that to copy powers you don't have with ones you do.<br /> <br /> Focusing on how many powers are listed on a character sheet isn't indicative of how much power a character actually has. A Green Lantern technically only has the one power: access to a power ring. That still has an obnoxious amount of variety, flexibility and redundancy to it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Well, what can I say? </div> </div> </div>What help you actually want.<br /> <br /> You asked for thoughts/opinions. Mine are that you're trying to push Mage so far past what it's good at doing that you should start looking at other games. Not because those games are going to be perfect for you, but because spending 50 hours hammering Mage from 25% of what you want to 50% of what you want seems silly when you could find a game where you could tinker for 5 hours to go from 75% of what you want to 95% of what you want.<br /> <br /> As noted, this also gets frustrating because you're willing to voice certain likes/dislikes very assertively, but for some reason aren't paying attention to others, and are reacting defensively to having that pointed out. A huge amount of the design issues you're bringing up about Scion 2e could be leveled just as much, or more so, at Mage, but Mage has... something... that seems to have you wanting to work on it instead.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T17:52:07-07:00","id":1477142,"title":"","content":"Honestly it looks to me as if you'd be better off playing one of the superhero games like Champions or Mutants and Masterminds that have the sort of power scale and optional lack of vulnerabilities you seem to want, and just focusing the campaign on mysticism rather than 4-color superheroics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T19:54:40-07:00","id":1477151,"title":"","content":"I believe a big problem with those is that (like Aberrant to an extent) it's very hard to do &quot;big&quot; broad powers. Making Superman requires taking dozens of individual specific powers. Making a Green Lantern generally involves extremely convoluted powers if you don't go the &quot;build everything as individual powers,&quot; route. Most supers games can't be distilled down to a few broad powers like Mage's Spheres (or as I'm trying to argue Scion 2e's Purviews).<br /> <br /> Supers games that do go for broader powers tend to be more narrative systems (like Masks in PbtA or Atomic Robo for Fate) which I'm not getting the impression are Irioth's speed.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T20:00:56-07:00","id":1477154,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477116#post1477116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Serious question: Do you care about the utility of the game as a group activity? Because a lot of your dislikes seem to be around things that ensure everyone playing has their own things.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, I do not mean to intrude on other characters' established roles if they have been actually set up in the group and being fulfilled by team-mates in play. But I like to be a master of many things, and be able to fulfill all those roles in the contingency that others are not actually filling the shoes. I like to play Superman, and when the Justice League is doing its thing, I am not stealing the speedster's role from Flash. In the hypothetical that he is incapacitated or I am in my solo series, I cherish the thought of being able to use super-speed. I am intolerant of the author's attempt to prevent me from having super-speed in my character sheet because someone else might be a speedster. I also cherish the thought that my abilities are inherent, not tied to a tool like Green Lantern, and nobody can make me helpless by stealing my power ring.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also feels very strange to hate on thematic constraints when that's pretty much what 99% of Mage is built on.. </div> </div> </div>Well, the thematic constraints of Mage act more like a lens than a barrier if you know what you are doing, and typically don't stop me from being able to do anything I want, including all the typical uses of magic I fancy. I do not mind the character-creation intellectual exercise of setting up a set of paradigm, practices, and instruments in these terms. The thematic constraints I dislike are more like you can be an Elementalist or a White Mage or a Mentalist, when I want to be all three. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Purviews are extremely broad powers. They don't have dot ratings like Spheres. Once you get access to a Purview you get the whole package. Marvels from one Purview can copy any power from any other Purview with the minor thematic constraint of, &quot;explain how it fits one of your available Motifs.&quot; In Ascension, you have to explain how an effect fits your Focus just to us the powers you already have, Scion 2e only makes you do that to copy powers you don't have with ones you do. </div> </div> </div>We shall see. I have no reason to doubt your word, but I do not really mean to act on Scion while the game is still in an incomplete state, especially with the God section being missing. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Focusing on how many powers are listed on a character sheet isn't indicative of how much power a character actually has. A Green Lantern technically only has the one power: access to a power ring. That still has an obnoxious amount of variety, flexibility and redundancy to it. </div> </div> </div>Bad example for me since the power ring issue makes GL a no-no for me, but if you redefine it as Silver Surfer I see your point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You asked for thoughts/opinions. Mine are that you're trying to push Mage so far past what it's good at doing that you should start looking at other games. Not because those games are going to be perfect for you, but because spending 50 hours hammering Mage from 25% of what you want to 50% of what you want seems silly when you could find a game where you could tinker for 5 hours to go from 75% of what you want to 95% of what you want.<br /> <br /> As noted, this also gets frustrating because you're willing to voice certain likes/dislikes very assertively, but for some reason aren't paying attention to others, and are reacting defensively to having that pointed out. A huge amount of the design issues you're bringing up about Scion 2e could be leveled just as much, or more so, at Mage, but Mage has... something... that seems to have you wanting to work on it instead. </div> </div> </div>Really, remolding Mage to my preferences did not really take me anywhere close to the time and effort you imply, nor I would say the rule changes yielded that limited a benefit for my preferences. As it concerns selling me Scion 2e, if it doesn't really pique my interest, it does not pique my interest. I know you are trying to be helpful, and I am thankful for that, but love cannot be forced. Perhaps when I have the whole game, and probably when I am able to start with playing at the OP level I fancy, things might change, or they might not. What I know is that Origin and Hero made an awful job of getting me interested. The other game definitely got me fascinated from the beginning, despite the many things I soon decided were in dire need of houseruling (e.g. 1st ed foci rules were absolutely horrible, and I never tolerated Vulgar w/o Witnesses).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T20:15:08-07:00","id":1477157,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477151#post1477151\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I believe a big problem with those is that (like Aberrant to an extent) it's very hard to do &quot;big&quot; broad powers. Making Superman requires taking dozens of individual specific powers. Making a Green Lantern generally involves extremely convoluted powers if you don't go the &quot;build everything as individual powers,&quot; route. Most supers games can't be distilled down to a few broad powers like Mage's Spheres (or as I'm trying to argue Scion 2e's Purviews).<br /> <br /> Supers games that do go for broader powers tend to be more narrative systems (like Masks in PbtA or Atomic Robo for Fate) which I'm not getting the impression are Irioth's speed.</div> </div> </div> </div>You have a point, although in my experience, generous use of Arrays, or whatever is the equivalent in Champions, and unlimited power points can mitigate the problem to some extent (albeit within reasonable limits unless you end up being one of those silly comic-book characters that can only use one superpower at a time). Sadly Aberrant never came around to use the same tool. You are right that I prefer the crunchy side of the superhero rpg spectrum. I have yet to find a superhero RPG that fits my tastes more than Mutants &amp; Masterminds, although I am extremely fond of Aberrant's setting (Teragen Forever!!).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-23-2022, 11:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T20:22:07-07:00","id":1477158,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477142#post1477142\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly it looks to me as if you'd be better off playing one of the superhero games like Champions or Mutants and Masterminds that have the sort of power scale and optional lack of vulnerabilities you seem to want, and just focusing the campaign on mysticism rather than 4-color superheroics.</div> </div> </div> </div>Been there, done that, albeit M&amp;M rather than Champions. I usually love the intellectual exercise of optimized character creation, and hence crunchy games with lots of options, but within reasonable limits. I want to design a Superman or Silver Surfer expy, not the blueprints for the Space Shuttle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-23-2022, 11:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T21:12:18-07:00","id":1477164,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1949\" data-vbnamecard=\"1949\">Irioth</a> You seem to be avoiding a lot of my points/questions that revolve around the &quot;game&quot; aspects in all this. I'm really not sure what else to say at this point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-23T23:05:11-07:00","id":1477171,"title":"","content":"Here's the thing:<br /> <br /> You don't like Mage: the Ascension. Period. You like the Storytelling System and Mage's magic system, not the game, because you do not fancy its actual proposal.<br /> <br /> I'm particularly baffled that you didn't ditch it completely for Mutants and Masterminds. I think M&amp;M 2nd Ed + Ultimate Power fits you perfectly. You won't ever need anything else.<br /> <br /> Now, all that said, if you really want to do this with Mage, the book you want is <i>World of Darkness: </i><i>Mirrors</i>, one of the last books from 1st ed CofD (when it was still called nWoD). It is precisely about hacking those games to your hearts content without a care for the canon setting, and one of its examples even is a crossover setting that plays out as a comic-book universe just using the rules for the games and tweaking them enough to go for a very OP and openly flashy game.<br /> <br /> All that said, the question about what you think of the game as a group activity is relevant. There's nothing inherently wrong with liking the game just as a solitary thought experiment, but knowing that helps us to understand how to best address your proposals. Both to help you and to not get frustrated ourselves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T03:47:52-07:00","id":1477186,"title":"","content":"Ahh, I see the issue. OK, a little background disclosure on my part. Some time ago, with my group we dabbled with various games, notably including Ars Magica and the WoD (modern and Dark Ages versions). Being the guy with the largest RPG collection as well as the greatest expertise, I was a player that was also assistant ST in charge of rule and setting issues for most games, again including ArM and the WoD. My friend was main ST in charge of the story. He and the other players almost always deferred to my judgement about such issues, so I had close to a free hand hacking and customizing the games the way I deemed best.This is when I got to use a few of the rule hacks we have been discussing (and others concerning other games and WoD splats, but this is an issue for another day), such as no Vulgar w/o witnesses, mages and fae soaking lethal damage, and rolling Arete + Sphere, without any serious difficulty or controversy.<br /> <br /> More recently, I have been involved in solo play, including a multiverse meta-setting (Jumpchain) where you 'jump' between universes and spend time in various fiction settings. It is solo play, although there are online communities where you can share ideas and experiences, as well as guideline documents about the worlds. The list of the worlds I use (typically OP fantasy and superheroic franchises) includes the Aberrant, ArM, WoD, and CoD settings. Being solo play with permissive rules to begin with, of course you get near-complete leeway to customize the worlds to your preferences.<br /> <br /> Options in the jump document for the WoD gave me the idea to remold the WoD into an apocalyptic setting where the Ascension War turned Hot and engulfed the other supernaturals. Reorganizing them into Triatic uber.factions was my own idea. I picked further inspiration from various other sources, including the Ascension and Apocalypse books. WoD: Mirrors probably played a part at an unconscious level, but I had forgotten about the book's existence and features. For all my experience being the rules and setting expert for my old group, my RPG collection is huge, including most WoD and CoD books. Therefore, sometimes being mindful where a particular rule or setting bit can be found is not easy.<br /> <br /> Hence, I have experience with playing the WoD as a group and solo play, and I am accustomed to be the guy who decides how the rules and setting should work in both cases.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-24-2022, 07:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T10:43:36-07:00","id":1477233,"title":"","content":"While increasing insight into your perspective is useful, it's also not really answering two questions I asked:<br /> <br /> 1) Do you care about the utility of the game as a group activity?<br /> 2) What help [do] you actually want[?]","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T12:40:17-07:00","id":1477259,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477233#post1477233\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While increasing insight into your perspective is useful, it's also not really answering two questions I asked:<br /> <br /> 1) Do you care about the utility of the game as a group activity?<br /> 2) What help [do] you actually want[?]</div> </div> </div> </div>1) Well, I am not sure I understand your question here. Group play sure is better for various reasons but solo play is acceptable too and good in its own way (and jumpchain is lots of wish-fulfilment fun). When it is the former I do not mean to step on anyone else's fun, but I demand the same courtesy, and I am accustomed to have ample leeway about rule and setting variables. As far as I am concerned, my enthusiastic hacking is not so much a group issue. Rather it is willful disregard of canon when it gets in the way and being confident of knowing better than any author, at least as it concerns rule and setting bits (I am admittably terrible with creative writing). It is not even an issue limited to TTRPG; I am just an enthusiastic hacker of computer games, at least when I get the necessary technical knowledge or software tools.<br /> <br /> 2) Well, I kind of expected technical advice on the fine details of the hacks I did. E.g. I am determined to make human-like supernaturals as resilient as the other Night-Folk, but I am uncertain whether to give the sturdiest mortals the same courtesy (although I am sure I want mages and changelings sturdier than average Muggles, and/or avoid grandmas that shrug off knives and bullets). I do want to make mages more resilient to Acclimation and Disembodiment, but I am uncertain whether to reverse things to the M20 optional best standard, or do away with the problem entirely.<br /> <br /> By the way, and on second thoughts, I object to the assumption that doing away with Vulgar without Witnesses or rolling Arete + Sphere makes mages too powerful. That was the standard in DA:M (and DA:F worked under very similar premises), and Tradition magic was almost entirely Coincidental in MtSC if you but paid any attention to RP foci and paradigm. In my knowledge and experience things worked fine, and hence you can transplant the same conditions to modern times provided you keep things hidden from Sleepers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-24-2022, 04:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-24T13:48:25-07:00","id":1477266,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477259#post1477259\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Well, I am not sure I understand your question here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is a critique such as, &quot;this rule works for the purpose of making mages stronger, but would negatively impact group play,&quot; meaningful to you?<br /> <br /> To use a complete hypothetical example: &quot;Flying Bricks in M&amp;M start at PL 24, and all other PCs start at PL 12.&quot;<br /> <br /> This certainly works to make flying bricks more powerful, but it's not great for group play because either (a) everyone makes PL 24 flying bricks which is a vat of redundancy and hard to manage for everyone, or (b) one player makes a flying brick and everyone else is a sidekick to them at best and only gets to do anything important at the beneficence of the flying brick player (which is a sucky place to be even if the flying brick player is usually good about sharing spotlight).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When it is the former I do not mean to step on anyone else's fun, but I demand the same courtesy, and I am accustomed to have ample leeway about rule and setting variables. </div> </div> </div>Based on your own descriptions of things, and your discussions of various systems, it is very hard not to feel like the leeway you were given has wildly skewed your perspective on this subject.<br /> <br /> For a game to be effective as a group activity, it has to put limits on characters to keep all the characters in roughly the same mechanical and narrative scale. You seem to be treating the limits that are designed to keep the players on an even field as limits on your fun. If you don't care about the rules as being useful for group play? This doesn't actually matter. If you do want the rules to work for group play, maintaining these sorts of limitations is really important to getting the game to work.<br /> <br /> Hence it's really important for us to get you to answer this more directly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 2) Well, I kind of expected technical advice on the fine details of the hacks I did. </div> </div> </div>OK. One of the reasons I don't post on the RPG.net design forum as much as I used to, is that I just can't stand screaming at my computer, &quot;We can't tell you because there's no context!&quot; when people post these sorts of things. It gets inanely frustrating to constantly see people ask, &quot;is this a good idea?&quot; without enough context or framework to have any idea how to answer. Is that rule good? It wildly varies based on lots of factors, and especially on what the goal is.<br /> <br /> It's impossible to get into the fine details of the house rule to get rid of vulgar without witnesses with the level of context as it currently stands. Same with things like soaking more types of damage, or larger casting pools, or whatever. These things can't be examined in a vacuum. Have you made mages more powerful? Yes. That's basically all we can say with what you've given us.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> E.g. I am determined to make human-like supernaturals as resilient as the other Night-Folk, but I am uncertain whether to give the sturdiest mortals the same courtesy (although I am sure I want mages and changelings sturdier than average Muggles, and/or avoid grandmas that shrug off knives and bullets). </div> </div> </div>As resilient as which other Night-Folk? There's a complete spectrum from as &quot;fragile&quot; as mages and changelings, to can recover (with enough time) from anything short of complete atomic disintegration. How do gaps in resilience factor into the calculus? Werewolves are quite resilient, except for silver which they're quite fragile against. How does a given Night-folk's ability to increase their resilience beyond baseline factor in? Vampires have a pretty good baseline toughness (baring their weakness), but Fortitude is one of the weakest Disciplines for what it does and what it costs. Considering that cost against what Life and Matter can do in terms of boosting your Soak and making normal clothing into super-armor (and all the other stuff you get to do along side that), are mages really behind vampires in the total picture of things?<br /> <br /> Fine details requires a more nuanced premise to discuss than, &quot;vampires and werewolves get to soak lethal damage as innate bonuses, so mages should too.&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T03:33:13-07:00","id":1477322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477266#post1477266\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is a critique such as, &quot;this rule works for the purpose of making mages stronger, but would negatively impact group play,&quot; meaningful to you?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not really, because everyone gets to play one kind of mage or another, which all get to benefit equally from the rule changes, or at least other kinds of supernaturals, which also get their own boosts. IIRC, in my old group changelings got similar kinds of benefits as mages, which made them rather more comparable to DA: F fae. C20 was yet to come, and I still have to make a detailed analysis of C20 and comparison with DA:F to decide what tweaks, if any, besides better soak would be warranted besides getting a better soak. However, the purpose is to make the fae roughly similar to mages, in the past and in modern times. Vampires also got their own boost, mostly in the terms of making access to improved Generation without Diablerie relatively easy, be it in the terms of naturally occurring with time and Xp (kinda like in Requiem) and/or through special mystical rituals. IIRC there was one canon example for the latter in VtM 1st ed, and I made it more common. Werewolves are already optimized as killing machines in any iteration of the WoD, so I do not remember if we changed anything in their favor or dunno if any would be necessary in modern times.<br /> <br /> You see, the purpose of my rule changes is not so much to make mages and fae invincible for other supernaturals, although I am fine with them being top of the pecking order. This also because there is a quality vs. quantity balance: vampires and shapeshifters are easier to make than mages and fae. Moreover, other supernaturals have their own ways of resisting magic, and means of dealing damage or mental manipulation that may take special effort to withstand. The purpose is to make them stronger against the world and mundane NPCs, and effectively invincible except to their own kind, other kinds of Night-Folk, or use of their special weaknesses. I loathe the Conan story scenario of a mundane pulling a sword or a gun on an unprepared wizard and dispatching them with relative ease, and I mean to make it unfeasible.<br /> <br /> In my experience, nobody ever plays a mundane, so there is no problem with making them helpless cannon fodder to the Night-Folk. Mages and fae eventually went into hiding because of the Technocracy and Paradox/Banality, vampires because exploiting their weaknesses is relatively easy, and weres because (handwave). Other kinds of supernaturals are rare enough that they can be dealt with on a case by case basis. I loathe the concept of Hunters/Inquisitors, so I shunned the Hunter game line, although I picked DA:I for completist purposes and knowing the enemy. Thankfully nobody ever tried to play one at my table. If they had really wanted, I suppose they could have been dealt with by making them another kind of supernatural with a false conscience, as Dark Ages did. This would also be my preferred solution if someone ever wants to play a mundane.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Based on your own descriptions of things, and your discussions of various systems, it is very hard not to feel like the leeway you were given has wildly skewed your perspective on this subject. </div> </div> </div>Probably, but it is a status quo I have grown quite fond of and I am very reluctant to give up. I am OK with limiting my group play to people that are willing to grant me the same kind of leeway, or sticking to solo play and stuff like Jumpchain if I can't find any.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For a game to be effective as a group activity, it has to put limits on characters to keep all the characters in roughly the same mechanical and narrative scale. You seem to be treating the limits that are designed to keep the players on an even field as limits on your fun. If you don't care about the rules as being useful for group play? This doesn't actually matter. If you do want the rules to work for group play, maintaining these sorts of limitations is really important to getting the game to work. </div> </div> </div>I suppose the bit above may apply, but in any case, this concern is not a priority for me, but I am open-minded to give it some heed if it does not interfere with my goals and preferences. I typically prefer to level the playing field upwards rather than downwards for various kinds of 'special' PC, and I do not really care if this makes the situation unsustainable for Muggle NPC. Speaking in D&amp;D-style terms, my preferred solution to magic-users being so damn effective (and even more so with the tweaks I am going to apply, such as doing away with Vancian magic) is to grant the PC warriors all kinds of supernatural martial-arts techniques that make them the effective equivalent of the spell-slingers, as manga/anime fantasy typically does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As resilient as which other Night-Folk? There's a complete spectrum from as &quot;fragile&quot; as mages and changelings, to can recover (with enough time) from anything short of complete atomic disintegration. How do gaps in resilience factor into the calculus? Werewolves are quite resilient, except for silver which they're quite fragile against. How does a given Night-folk's ability to increase their resilience beyond baseline factor in? Vampires have a pretty good baseline toughness (baring their weakness), but Fortitude is one of the weakest Disciplines for what it does and what it costs. Considering that cost against what Life and Matter can do in terms of boosting your Soak and making normal clothing into super-armor (and all the other stuff you get to do along side that), are mages really behind vampires in the total picture of things? </div> </div> </div>As I said, the purpose of the rule is to make mages and changelings as resilient as vampires and werewolves to mundane weapons even if they made zero defensive preparation. No Muggle should ever pull a gun or a sword on a supernatural and expect to come out the winner or even alive, even if the Night-Folk was caught with their pants down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-25-2022, 11:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T15:10:52-07:00","id":1477364,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477322#post1477322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not really,...</div> </div> </div> </div>Then, again, what kind of help or responses are your really looking for? You know what you're doing makes mages more powerful, and the &quot;right kind of powerful&quot; is your personal vision that none of the rest of us have complete knowledge and understanding of.<br /> <br /> You have a group that enjoys it? Great. You acknowledge lots of other groups aren't going to want it and don't mind? Great. What's left to be said?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This also because there is a quality vs. quantity balance: </div> </div> </div>This sort of thing is frustrating for me though, because when I bring up comparative balance issues beyond innate powers, they don't count, but you want to take ease of supernaturals reproducing into account?<br /> <br /> How are we supposed to know what factors you care about, and which you don't? If this is really about mages vs. the mundane, why does this matter at all?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ...or use of their special weaknesses. </div> </div> </div>Mages don't have special weaknesses. Their &quot;weakness&quot; is that without magic they're basically just human, and you're trying to eliminate that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> As I said, the purpose of the rule is to make mages and changelings as resilient as vampires and werewolves to mundane weapons even if they made zero defensive preparation. No Muggle should ever pull a gun or a sword on a supernatural and expect to come out the winner or even alive, even if the Night-Folk was caught with their pants down. </div> </div> </div>So, the problem here, is the base system you're using sucks for this. For all you're hyping up vampires and werewolves in the WoD... they're not actually that tough.<br /> <br /> Consider a supernatural coming around a corner into a dark alley only to get instantly stabbed by a mugger laying in wait for the next person to come long.<br /> <br /> A vampires with Stamina 1 is actually worse off than a mage with Stamina 5, despite the vampire's soak applying and the mage's not. 1 die of soak isn't great, and Stamina 1 leaves the vampire extremely open to getting Stunned, while the mage is extremely unlikely to get Stunned. So the vampire is at serious risk of being forced to just stand that trying to spend WP and Vitae to do anything at all before their assailant stabs them into torpor, while the mage is at a slightly higher risk of death if they don't act fast, but they can actually toss everything they have into a spell to do something about the situation because they're not stun-locked.<br /> <br /> These things are more complicated than what kinds of damage you can soak (and lets not get into the complexity of the Garou and different soaking by forms).<br /> <br /> I think one of the reasons a lot of us are somewhat baffled by the insistence on sticking to the WoD, is that there are already systems that better facilitate this sort of idea.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T16:47:33-07:00","id":1477372,"title":"","content":"<span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span>, drawing from other game systems to make Mages less squishy...<br /> <br /> There is a CRPG called, <i>Torment: Tides of Numinera</i>. Oom, an egg shaped creature you can add to your party, has the ability to <a href=\"https://tormenttidesofnumenera.wiki.fextralife.com/Haptic+Armor\" target=\"_blank\">wrap a portion of its body around the main character</a>, granting an armor bonus. <br /> <br /> In my own campaign, I have the possibility of Umbrood Symbiants, which go inside the host's body, substituting their essence for the host's health levels when damage is taken, at the cost of Quintessence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thank you for passing time with me in conversation. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1480242-horizon-s-wild-and-crazy-homebrew-rules-and-metaplot-choices\" target=\"_blank\">My Hacks</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26467-horizonparty2021","name":"HorizonParty2021","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26467_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"610"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T18:49:31-07:00","id":1477381,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1949\" data-vbnamecard=\"1949\">Irioth</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> finally managed to help you talk thehe things we needed to know to help you, at least most them, so let me throw some considerations and suggestions.<br /> <br /> 1 - For one, you'll need to learn <b>a lot</b> to deal with other people not just giving you the same leeway you're used to if you want at least to have some meaningful conversations on the internet. That's probably why no one gave attention to your suggestions for Scion: your suggestions completely ignore what other people want out of the game, and the game must be for everyone, not just yourself.<br /> <br /> Think about this: as is, Mage worked for you even if you feel the need to hack it, because it already has pieces you enjoy. If it were already like you want, though, II can confidently say most people here wouldn't had ever be interested in the game. You can't expect to be taken seriously or get anything from people if you don't pay some heed to their own wants and needs, even if after that you do things your own way at your table. This is relevant, if for no other reasons, to know how to ask, how to be understood, and how to compromise when, like in the Scion case, you need something from a project that's not meant just for you.<br /> <br /> All this is minor stuff, but it did got in the way of communication here, so just for you to understand why it took us 2 whole pages just to get your point.<br /> <br /> 2 - Rules in an RPG may serve a lot of purposes, some are for thematic purposes, they help enforce the themes and feel of the game instead of being there for balance. On themselves, most of the rules you complain about are like that. In fact, the whole Paradox notion is thematic and can be dealt away as you see fit. I think you would like to read Mage the Awakening 2nd Edition (where Paradox isn't tied to vulgarity at all IIRC), but you can simply not apply it without balance concerns for the kind of game you want.<br /> <br /> 3 - Balance between splats is a little trickier if you want it to exist. In that case, the freeform Magic system is already too powerful and most of your hacks create a problem that is harder to solve than you think. Then, you shouldn't make Magic Arete + Something because the low Pool for improvised spells is one of the main balancing factors, and Paradox as something that hinders Mages in some situations is the next one. In that case I advise heavily against spellcasting being anything but Arete outside a ritual, and Paradox should be exploitable by other splats in some way, although you can tie it to something other than Consensus or Vulgarity.<br /> <br /> One option to counter such changes would be to thoroughly revise rules for Night Folk's Spell Resistance, so other supernaturals have an easier time and/or a harsher effect when they resist Magic.<br /> <br /> 4 - Damage and resilience poises another problem you're not considering in a multi-splat game. Forget about Muggles, the difference in resiliency is one of the main factors currently in the game to both differentiate splats, define their niches and balance them. As you make Mages more resistant you have to decide for the others if you want to keeo their niche as defined by being more resilient, then how to improve it if you do, or where to compensate it if you don't.<br /> <br /> One option is to change the rules on <b><i>natural damage sources</i></b> instead of on splat resilience, so mortals and things like falls and whatnot are less likely to ever harm a supernatural and they also die easier (you can change their rules for Health and soak), without touching how the splats actually differentiate themselves. I think this &quot;Mortal Splat&quot; that is weaker than baseline and applied to mundane people would work better for your purposes.<br /> <br /> 5 - While we're at that, though, Heavy Arms has a very salient point. The whole danger from common mortals that you loathe so much is actually a design purpose of the whole WoD system and setting, it is how it is designed to work and the system won't help you making the game less dangerous in actual fights. While it can be done, it will be a hack that takes too much effort when you could simply transplant the setting (or what you like of it) and the powers to other systems. Again, I think you would be better hacking WoD into M&amp;M 2nd Ed using the guidelines in Ultimate Power (I personally loathe M&amp;M 3rd Ed). I would even want to see it if you do it.<br /> <br /> You may like to look into Street Fighter RPG for the Storytelling System for a better take on the Health and Damage system for your purpose, it is official WW stuff even if it isn't canonical WoD material, and it can even help you find rules for hunters that you actually like (because they're not Muggles even if they're humans) while keeping Muggles weak. But I still think hacking the setting and splats into M&amp;M is more worth your time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-25T19:23:45-07:00","id":1477385,"title":"","content":"To clarify a bit on Awakening 2e:<br /> <br /> In that game, there is no &quot;vulgar&quot; magic. The only thing that matters is if Sleepers witness &quot;obvious&quot; magic. If Sleepers witness the active casting of obvious magic it increases the risk of Paradox (witnesses to obvious magic after it's cast deal with a different mechanic). Awakening 2e's approach to Paradox is much more focused on the concept of reaching beyond you safe limits. Mages in Awakening 2e have a &quot;safe&quot; number of Reaches they can apply to a spell to make it more powerful, but they can spend as many Reaches as they want, though this vastly increases the risk of Paradox.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-29T14:00:32-07:00","id":1477643,"title":"","content":"1. Yeah, communication flaws on the Net are a recurrent and significant problem of mine. Online I lack non-verbal clues to do course correction on the fly, so the self-centered, assertive, and/or defensive aspects often come out magnified and more belligerent than intended. I do not mean to antagonize people, and I am sad and sorry when it happens, but keeping the issue in check is easier said than done.<br /> <br /> I do not mean to spoil other people's fun, and I am willing to try and get along or seek constructive compromise on issues that do not violate my core playstyle preferences, but I am also unwilling to give much ground on the latter. E.g. with my old group we also did a few games which I had little interest in, but also did not find intolerable, such as Call of Chtulhu. I tried my best to get along with the plot, even if I am basically unable to get in tune with or RP the themes and emotions of horror, be it cosmic or personal. I can only relate to the horror genre through the lens of action-adventure or dark fantasy, so I specialized in playing the kind of fearless monster hunter or occultist that is willing to try and nuke Lovecraftian Abominations, and everyone had fun. OTOH, to be honest, I have grown unwilling to compromise on my core preferences, such as several ones discussed in this thread, since I find them necessary to my enjoyment. I'd be entirely willing to limit my group play to people that are willing and able to get along with my foibles, such as my old group did, or otherwise stick to solo play.<br /> <br /> As it concerns suggestions to try Scion 2e, ATM I am considering to try my hand at experimenting with Aberrant 2e, which IIUC shares much of the same system. I am more open-minded with Aberrant than Scion for various reasons. Otherwise, I'd stick with hacked WoD or CoD. Broadly speaking, I find good things and bad things in M20 and Awakening 2e, the good ones good enough reasons to make them two of my preferred games, the bad ones bad enough to make a few key hacks necessary and non-negotiable, even if details may vary. I find myself wavering back and forth between the two options over time.<br /> <br /> We discussed my hack preferences for M20 at some length. As it concerns MtAw 2e, I am rather intolerant of the rigid Path system and the related need to use Mana for most Arcana. However, the issue may be solved to my satisfaction by using a freeform Path system that lets me mix and match Ruling and Inferior Arcana freely, such as the Circle of Degrees official hack in Mage Chronicler\u2019s Guide, and a houserule Merit that lets me have three or four Ruling Arcana w/o need to lock down the Legacy slot for that (even if the ability to use a Legacy may be seriously compromised or lost). I once posted an example of this <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/906517-2e-homebrew-path-and-merit\">here</a>.<br /> <br /> 2. I understand that some of the rules I dislike may have thematic justification, but as a rule I have little interest and find scarce value in the themes they uphold. E.g. about Paradox I only find any value in it at all insofar as it keeps magic out of the notice of Sleepers, hence I may justify Vulgarity in the face of mundanes, but I loathe it when they are not around, because magic should be flashy, powerful, and doable in the field and on the fly by default. Subtle magic is only justifiable when it is so by its very nature, or when you need to fool present and active mundane notice. Ritual magic is only justifiable when you need the power boost, the spell goes above and beyond common magic feats, and/or the paradigm really calls for that (and I'd never play that kind of mage). I shun games or fiction where magic is an important element of the game or story but mostly or entirely subtle, slow, and/or ritual by nature, regardless of their other merits.<br /> <br /> 3: I remain skeptical that balance concerns about my intended rule hacks for M20 are that important or troublesome, also because they often have canon analogues in other versions and games. E,g, as you have pointed out, Vulgarity does not really exist in Awakening 2e, the Arete + Sphere roll is used in Dark Ages: Mage, Dark Ages: Fae uses something very similar for fae magic, and M20 has the Cinematic Damage rule. My main purpose in hacking the latter is to increase the power gap between human-like supernaturals and mortals. What was good enough for the authors of those rules is good enough for me. Transplanting MtAw 2e or DA:M magic system to the M20 setting may be an option, but also need some serious work: E.g. DA:M is useless for modern play unless you devise Foundations and Pillars for Akhasics, Ecstatics, Euthanatos, Etherites, Virtual Adepts, etc.<br /> <br /> 4. Suggestion to deal with the mage/fae vs. mortal power balance by weakening the latter may have a lot of merit, but at first glance needs a lot of design work to implement..<br /> <br /> 5: Honestly, the thematic elements that may underlie mortals being a significant potential threat for supernaturals or combat being dangerous enough that most supernaturals shun it do not interest me at all, and I find little value in them. As far as I am concerned, the various splats have their own weaknesses (mages have witness-only Paradox, changeling have Banality, vampires have their banes, etc.) and that should be enough for their enemies to exploit and to keep the Masquerade in place. As far as I am concerned, mortals that pick a fight with supernaturals, w/o extensive preparation should be doomed cannon fodder. I assume plenty of hacks should be feasible that make the Storyteller System more comfortable for combat-heavy play. I suppose at least a few of them have been published in some supplement or another down the years, even if ATM I am not mindful of any detail.<br /> <br /> The idea to hack the WoD with superheroic systems such M&amp;M 3ed or Aberrant 2e may have merit, but again it may need some serious consideration.and experimentation before plunging.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1949,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","user":"Irioth","date":". 03-30-2022, 02:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1949-irioth","name":"Irioth","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"566"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-29T17:26:27-07:00","id":1477654,"title":"","content":"It's worth noting that the Scale system that's featured in most of the Storypath games originated in the Chronicles of Darkness's <b>Mage Chronicler's Guide</b> back when it was still the \u201cnew World of Darkness\u201d; that book was basically a <b>Mage</b>-specific <b>Mirrors</b>, and Scaling was introduced in a section (pp.210\u2013219) about adapting <b>Mage: the Awakening</b> as a game about superheroes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-04-01T22:01:06-07:00","id":1477881,"title":"","content":"I'll try to keep it to some specifics.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477643#post1477643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I do not mean to spoil other people's fun, and I am willing to try and get along or seek constructive compromise on issues that do not violate my core playstyle preferences, but I am also unwilling to give much ground on the latter.</div> </div> </div> </div>You may benefit from loosening this on discussions. The point isn't to accept anything <i>on your game</i>, but to accept that something that violates your core preferences will make it to the general consensus/final product and have to be hacked by you.<br /> <br /> It is a social activity, but while your gaming group is restricted in numbers and as such easier to handle on the basis of just choosing to not play with people that don't share that preference, larger debates and game projects involve a too large number of people for this strategy to work. You either learn to somehow work with people that disagree with you on those principles, or you'll alienate yourself from every debate and project.<br /> <br /> On the personal difficulty to communicate, I can relate although in a rather reversed way, since I'm really bad at reading non-verbal cues and at giving them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477643#post1477643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I understand that some of the rules I dislike may have thematic justification, but as a rule I have little interest and find scarce value in the themes they uphold.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's precisely why I'm saying you don't need to worry with Paradox too much, at least outside splat crossovers (more on that later). Use Aw 2nd, or transplant it's Paradox rules to Asc, or just do away with Paradox entirely as you see fit.<br /> <br /> Actually Paradox is a very flexible rule precisely because it exists solely for thematic purposes and has no <b>mechanical</b> interaction of relevance with other rules, so you can simply make whatever you want with it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477643#post1477643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I remain skeptical that balance concerns about my intended rule hacks for M20 are that important or troublesome, also because they often have canon analogues in other versions and games.</div> </div> </div> </div>Been there, done that.<br /> <br /> As a rule of thumb WoD has very poor <b>mechanical</b> interaction between splats, and DA rules are even worse on that regard. But since you made a point about keeping instant magic as your main spellcasting method, then I agree on making it Arete + Something as a needed hack.<br /> <br /> On that case, though, if splat balance <b>is</b> a concern (i.e. you want balance on crossovers), I'd really recommend that you make other splats either be able to actively explore Paradox or have some sort of magic resistance/dispell on top of what's presented in the rules.<br /> <br /> Basically, Dice Pool Disparity is how Mage already handles Night Folk to make them dangerous opponents in Mage Core itself. While they have Static effects, those are free from Paradox and handled with greater dice pools, so it is an exchange of versatility for potency. If you take this potency away, you must give some other edge.<br /> <br /> Keep in mind that as is a Mage is already able to replicate and improve virtually any ability or power from other splats.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477643#post1477643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Suggestion to deal with the mage/fae vs. mortal power balance by weakening the latter may have a lot of merit, but at first glance needs a lot of design work to implement.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think it is an interesting avenue because it has less complex interactions afterwards, as it leaves the interactions between splats intact. Changes to the splats themselves will always change how they relate and may lead to further need to make changes on others so they keep their relative role. E.g.: Changers are exceptionally hard to damage in comparison to others, if you make Mages and Changelings also harder to damage then Changers cease being so above and you may, now or later, decide they also need more resilience to retake their role, but then damage sources that already exist will have a harder time doing anything to them, then you may, now or later, need to tweak them into causing more harm, and so on.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Irioth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1476949-anti-revised-metaplot-rules?p=1477643#post1477643\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, the thematic elements that may underlie mortals being a significant potential threat for supernaturals or combat being dangerous enough that most supernaturals shun it do not interest me at all, and I find little value in them.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but that was the point: since the system itself is built upon such premises it may not be mathematically supportive of less lethal combat, regardless of specific rules. But YMMV and you don't really need to shy from trying, it may work. Again, I think Street Fighter will help you with a good source for hacks into the core damage/health/recovery system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}